  "aciklama": "SS Arctic sank after a collision in fog off Newfoundland. The cause: hull damage below the waterline and lifeboats for fewer than half aboard.\n\nFailure chain: Full speed in fog → Collision with vesta → Hull holed → Run for land fails → Crew takes the boats\n\nRelated episode: Why USS Thresher Was Lost — https://youtu.be/8HybydJa-l8\n\nFootage & sources:\n• Archival film: \"Wreck of the U.S.M. steam ship \"Arctic\" (one-third-size).png\" — Wikimedia Commons (public domain / free licence): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wreck_of_the_U.S.M._steam_ship_%22Arctic%22_(one-third-size).png\n• Archival film: \"Loss of the U.S.M. steam ship Arctic- off Cape Race Wednesday September 27th 1854 LCCN2002709960.jpg\" — Wikimedia Commons (public domain / free licence): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loss_of_the_U.S.M._steam_ship_Arctic-_off_Cape_Race_Wednesday_September_27th_1854_LCCN2002709960.jpg\n• Archival film: \"SS Arctic collision map1854.jpeg\" — Wikimedia Commons (public domain / free licence): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SS_Arctic_collision_map1854.jpeg\n• Archival film: \"Wreck of the U.S.M. steam ship \"Arctic\" (half-size).png\" — Wikimedia Commons (public domain / free licence): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wreck_of_the_U.S.M._steam_ship_%22Arctic%22_(half-size).png\n• Archival film: \"'The Arctic' Steam-Ship - ILN 1854.jpg\" — Wikimedia Commons (public domain / free licence): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:'The_Arctic'_Steam-Ship_-_ILN_1854.jpg\n• Archival film: \"Loss of the Steam Ship Arctic.jpg\" — Wikimedia Commons (public domain / free licence): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loss_of_the_Steam_Ship_Arctic.jpg\n• Archival film: \"USM steamship Arctic (1850).jpg\" — Wikimedia Commons (public domain / free licence): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:USM_steamship_Arctic_(1850).jpg\n\nTechnical references:\n• Wikipedia — SS Arctic disaster — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Arctic_disaster\n• Government source — 1854&searchType=advanced&language=&sequence=0&words=Ar.,tic&proxdistan (cited by Wikipedia) — https://chroniclingamerica.loc.gov/lccn/sn83030213/1854-10-12/ed-1/seq-6/#date1=09/27/1854&index=9&date2=10/31/1854&searchType=advanced&language=&sequence=0&words=Ar.,tic&proxdistance=5&state=New+York&rows=20&ortext=Artic&proxtext=&phrasetext=&andtext=&dateFilterType=range&page=1\n\nNarration uses a synthetic voice. Footage is real archival film or licensed stock; anything reconstructed is labelled on screen.\n\nFailure File · Episode 30 · A new engineering failure, reconstructed every day.\n\n#shorts #engineering #SSArctic #maritime #history",
